@page "/characters"
@page "/build/characters"
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<PageTitle>Your characters — Prowlers &amp; Paragons</PageTitle>

@* Every character this browser or this account is holding, with the room to draw them all.

   **It is its own area now, `Area.Characters`, rather than a page under `/build`.** It used to be
   `Area.Play` by construction — `Areas.Of` reads the first path segment, so `/build/characters`
   inherited the step band and the budget strip whether or not either belonged. Neither did: the
   roster is not a creation step, and the block it draws above the list is the *open* character's
   own spend, read from the session exactly as the panel always has — not a fact the strip needed
   to repeat in a second format. Giving the roster its own area is what lets `Where` stop calling
   it a step rather than special-casing it out of the step band one component at a time.

   **The old address still answers, and it is a redirect rather than a second copy of this page.**
   `@@page "/build/characters"` above keeps a bookmark from breaking; `OnInitialized` below moves
   the address bar to the canonical one the moment it renders, so `Areas.Of` — and the banner's
   `Characters` tab — agree on where this page lives whichever door a reader came in by.

   **The page is the panel and nothing else.** Every action on a character — open, read, discard,
   start another, import — is `CharacterManager`'s, and a second control here would be a second
   place to keep in step. The heading is what a page owes a reader that a panel inside one does
   not. *@
<h1>Your characters</h1>
<p class="muted">Everything you have built, and the games they are in.</p>

<CharacterManager />

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// A visit through the old address moves the bar to the new one.
    ///
    /// <para><b>A client-side redirect, not a second live page.</b> Both <c>@@page</c> routes
    /// render this same component, so without this the roster would simply answer at two
    /// addresses forever and <c>Areas.Of("build/characters")</c> would keep reporting
    /// <see cref="Area.Play"/> for a page that has stopped being one. <c>replace: true</c> so the
    /// old address does not sit in browser history as a step back to itself.</para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        if (string.Equals(Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri), "build/characters",
                StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
        {
            Nav.NavigateTo("characters", replace: true);
        }
    }
}
